//````
# BDP Core — `core-syntax.lumen`

Single Lumen source for the BDP **Core** profile (v0.2). The toolchain backends
project it to the JSON Schema bundle, the OpenAPI catalog, Go interfaces, the
merged reference and the canonical CLI spec. There is no hand-written v0.2 schema
bundle today — Eva's `specs/core.md` carries the shapes as prose — so this file
becomes the one place the schemas are written, and core.md keeps the semantics.

Two standing rules, both Donna's:

1. **Only schema, no nominal types.** Every shape is a v0.2 `schema` — transparent,
   minting no identity (`schema X = T ⇒ X ≡ T`). Never `type X is …`.
2. **The core primitives are commands and functions**, marked **primitives, not
   callables** via `bdp = { kind = "primitive" }`.

Source: gastownhall/bdp @ `spec/core-v0.2`. Section references (§n) point into
core.md. `TODO(v0.2)` marks a shape still to reconcile, and `OPEN` marks something
core.md itself leaves open (§15).
//````

//````
## Overview

A BDP service holds a **Scope** of Resources and answers a fixed, small protocol
over them. A Resource is a **Bead** (a node) or a **Link** (a directed edge); the
two share one identifier space and one record shape, and `kind` discriminates them
(§2.2). Everything else — Types, the typed `createBead`/`updateLink` entry points,
query, batch — is a **callable** layered above this Core, not part of it (§6, §14).

The whole surface is six primitives, uniform over resource kind:

| primitive | method | what it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `get` | `GET` | safe point read of a Resource → its representation |
| `create` | `POST` | admit a new Bead or Link into a Scope |
| `update` | `PATCH` | JSON Patch a Resource's `/properties` and `/metadata` |
| `delete` | `DELETE` | retire a Resource; returns its final identity |
| `pull` | `GET` | read a page of a Resource stream (the changefeed or an EventSource) |
| `call` | `POST` | invoke a named callable and get its `Reply` back |

## Cross-op semantics

**One record, read everywhere.** `get`, `create` and `update` all answer with a
`ResourceRepresentation` — the same Bead/Link record that rides on events and in an
export. A record describes itself: it carries its own `kind`, `id`, `revision` and
`storageClass`, so a reader who met it on a stream needs no out-of-band framing
(§2.2).

**Two failure channels, split by primitive (§4.4, §4.5).** Only `call` carries the
rich `Outcome`. A callable that runs and reaches a verdict answers `success` or
`failure` (its own declared error); anything the *protocol* reports — a refused
request, a broken-off operation, a timeout — is a `fault` carrying an RFC 9457
`Problem`. The other five primitives have **no** `failure` and no `Outcome`: each
either returns its value or faults. Over HTTP those five stay as plain as HTTP
allows — 2xx carries the bare value, a failing status carries the `Problem` (§13.2).

**Context rides beside every call (§4.3).** Request context (`cause`, `provenance`,
`metadata`) and reply context (`cause`, `transaction`, `metadata`) travel in headers
for the five data primitives and inside the `Reply` for `call`. Context is never part
of a value.

**Revisions are opaque (§3.1).** A `revision` is an equality token, not an ordering
or a timestamp. `update` and `delete` take an optional `If-Match` revision for
optimistic concurrency; a mismatch faults with `revision-mismatch`.

**Events are the system's own report (§7).** Each Scope has a built-in **changefeed**
EventSource whose events are the change events `create`/`update`/`delete` produce.
`pull` reads any EventSource, the changefeed included, as finite pages over an opaque
cursor.
//````

//````
## Operations

The six primitives, documented as HTTP/REST. The shared rules — kind-uniformity, the
value-or-fault model, and the context headers — are in **Cross-op semantics** above,
not repeated here.
//````

/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "get" }
/// doc = { style = "http" }
/// description: Safe point read. Resolves a Reference to the current Resource representation, or a pinned revision when one is given. Never mutates; faults with 404/410 when the Resource is absent or erased.
/// openapi = {
///   operationId = "get", method = "GET", pathItem = "resource", tags = ["Core"],
///   paramGroups = ["requestContext", "replyContext"],
///   params = { revision = { in = "query", schema = "Revision", required = false } },
///   success = { status = 200, contentType = "application/json" },
///   problem = { status = [400, 404, 406, 410], contentType = "application/problem+json" }
/// }
fn get(resource: Reference): ResourceRepresentation

/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "create" }
/// doc = { style = "http" }
/// description: Admit a new Bead or Link into a Scope. The body is uniform over kind; the service allocates identity unless the caller proposes one (§5.4). Returns the admitted representation and its canonical URL in `Location`.
/// openapi = {
///   operationId = "create", method = "POST", pathItem = "scope", tags = ["Core"],
///   paramGroups = ["requestContext", "replyContext"],
///   request = { contentType = "application/json" },
///   success = { status = 201, contentType = "application/json" },
///   responseHeaders = { Location = { schema = "AbsoluteHttpUrl" } },
///   problem = { status = [400, 409, 422], contentType = "application/problem+json" }
/// }
command create(scope: Reference, draft: CreateRequest) returns ResourceRepresentation fails Problem

/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "update" }
/// doc = { style = "http" }
/// description: Apply a JSON Patch limited to `/properties` and `/metadata` (§3.3). Optional `If-Match` gives optimistic concurrency; a stale revision faults with `revision-mismatch`. Returns the new representation.
/// openapi = {
///   operationId = "update", method = "PATCH", pathItem = "resource", tags = ["Core"],
///   paramGroups = ["requestContext", "replyContext"],
///   params = { ifMatch = { in = "header", schema = "Revision", required = false } },
///   request = { contentType = "application/json-patch+json" },
///   success = { status = 200, contentType = "application/json" },
///   problem = { status = [400, 404, 409, 412, 422], contentType = "application/problem+json" }
/// }
command update(resource: Reference, patch: JsonPatch) returns ResourceRepresentation fails Problem

/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "delete" }
/// doc = { style = "http" }
/// description: Retire a Resource, minting a final revision. Idempotent — deleting an already-deleted Resource settles the same way. Faults with `incident-links-exist` when Links still attach (§5.6). Returns the final identity, not a representation.
/// openapi = {
///   operationId = "delete", method = "DELETE", pathItem = "resource", tags = ["Core"],
///   paramGroups = ["requestContext", "replyContext"],
///   params = { ifMatch = { in = "header", schema = "Revision", required = false } },
///   success = { status = 200, contentType = "application/json" },
///   problem = { status = [400, 404, 409, 412], contentType = "application/problem+json" }
/// }
command delete(resource: Reference) returns ResourceIdentity fails Problem idempotent

/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "pull" }
/// doc = { style = "http" }
/// description: Read a finite page of an EventSource — the changefeed or a declared stream. Resumes from an opaque cursor or a fresh start point; `head` tells a reader whether it has caught up. Safe; faults with `cursor-expired` below the retention floor (§7.5).
/// openapi = {
///   operationId = "pull", method = "GET", pathItem = "eventSource", tags = ["Core"],
///   paramGroups = ["requestContext", "replyContext"],
///   params = {
///     after = { in = "query", schema = "Cursor", required = false },
///     from = { in = "query", schema = "Revision", required = false },
///     limit = { in = "query", schema = "number", required = false },
///     representation = { in = "query", schema = "EventRepresentation", required = false }
///   },
///   success = { status = 200, contentType = "application/json" },
///   problem = { status = [400, 410], contentType = "application/problem+json" }
/// }
command pull(eventSource: Reference, request: PullRequest) returns PullPage fails Problem safe

/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "call" }
/// doc = { style = "http" }
/// description: Invoke a named callable. The only primitive with the rich Outcome — the returned `Reply` carries `success`, `failure` (the callable's declared error) or `fault`, and the client branches on the arm, not the status. May run async, returning a result bead at `Location` (§6.4/§6.5).
/// openapi = {
///   operationId = "call", method = "POST", pathItem = "callable", tags = ["Core"],
///   paramGroups = ["requestContext"],
///   params = { mode = { in = "query", schema = "CallMode", required = false } },
///   request = { contentType = "application/json" },
///   responses = [
///     { status = 200, contentType = "application/json", schema = "Reply", description = "sync — success Reply" },
///     { status = 409, contentType = "application/json", schema = "Reply", description = "declared failure or fault Reply (branch on the arm)" },
///     { status = 202, contentType = "application/json", schema = "AsyncResultBead", description = "async — result bead at Location" }
///   ],
///   responseHeaders = { Location = { schema = "AbsoluteHttpUrl" } }
/// }
fn call(callable: Reference, request: CallRequest): Reply

//````
## Built-in bead types

Beads fall into two families (§2.7). A **data bead** is an ordinary Resource, created,
updated and deleted through the primitives. A **capability bead** stands for something
the service can do; its storage class is `Intrinsic`, it is **declared by the service**
(a client cannot `create` one), and it answers `get` plus one of the capability
primitives.

Core names a small set of **well-known Types**, each identified by a fixed type-bead URL
so a client can recognise what a Bead is from its `type` alone. Core stays silent on what
each Type *means* — that is Types' job (§2.4) — except where a Type's content is one of
Core's own envelopes.

| Well-known Type | family | Core uses it to | Core says about its content |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `Type` | data | mark a type bead | nothing — Types defines it |
| `Callable` | capability | mark a Bead that answers `call` | nothing — Types defines the declaration |
| `EventSource` | capability | mark a Bead that answers `pull` | nothing — Types defines the declaration |
| `AsyncResult` | data | mark the result bead of an async `call` | that it holds a `Reply` (§6.5) — Core's own envelope |
| `ResultOf` | link | mark the Link from a result bead to the Callable it ran | nothing beyond its endpoints (§6.5) |

A Resource whose Type *refines* a well-known Type is treated as that Type; what "refines"
means is Types'. `AsyncResult` is the one exception to Core's silence about content,
because a result bead holds a `Reply`.

**OPEN (§15):** the fixed URLs of the well-known type beads are not yet assigned; a sixth
Type (`Alias`) is added if the §2.9 alias proposal stands; `ResultOf` is a placeholder
name.
//````

//````
## Primitive types

The scalar carriers the records are built from — base types plus the refinements
Lumen doesn't carry natively. Each is transparent (`schema X = T ⇒ X ≡ T`).
//````

/// bdp = { id = "https://github.com/gastownhall/bdp/schemas/bdp-v0.2#/$defs/bdpVersion" }
/// title: Protocol version
/// description: The Core profile version this document binds; a constant.
schema BdpVersion = "0.2"

/// title: Resource kind
/// description: Discriminates the two Resource shapes that share one identifier space.
schema ResourceKind = "bead" | "link"

/// title: Revision token
/// description: Nonempty opaque Resource-version token. Clients compare it for exact equality and cannot infer order, time or allocation from its spelling.
/// jsonschema = { minLength = 1 }
schema Revision = string

/// title: Timestamp
/// description: RFC 3339 date-time; uppercase T and Z.
/// jsonschema = { format = "date-time" }
schema DateTime = string

/// title: Local name
/// description: A short identifier token for a view or a well-known name; letters, digits, hyphen and underscore, leading letter.
/// jsonschema = { pattern = "^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*$" }
schema LocalName = string

/// title: Absolute URI
/// description: Any absolute URI; the identity form for Types and causes.
/// jsonschema = { format = "uri" }
schema AbsoluteUri = string

/// title: Resource URL
/// description: A Resource's absolute canonical identity URL; the local part is opaque to clients.
/// jsonschema = { format = "uri" }
schema AbsoluteHttpUrl = string

/// title: Idempotency key
/// description: Caller-chosen key that makes a retried write safe to collapse.
/// jsonschema = { pattern = "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,256}$" }
schema IdempotencyKey = string

/// title: Event ordinal
/// description: An event's position in its stream; monotone within one EventSource.
schema Ordinal = number

/// title: Stream cursor
/// description: Opaque resume token bound to one stream and the selection it came from; refused if used elsewhere. Also the point an export is cut at.
schema Cursor = string

/// title: Change kind
/// description: What a change event reports happened to its subject.
schema EventType = "created" | "updated" | "deleted"

/// title: Event representation
/// description: Whether `pull` returns each update's complete new state or a patch (§7.2).
schema EventRepresentation = "full" | "patch"

/// title: Call mode
/// description: Whether a callable is invoked synchronously or asynchronously (§6.4).
schema CallMode = "sync" | "async"

/// title: Identity basis
/// description: How a service established a principal's identity (§3.2).
schema IdentityBasis = "writer-supplied" | "channel-authenticated" | "unknown"

//````
## References and identity

A Reference is how one Resource names another: a floating URL, or a URL pinned to an
exact revision (§2.5).
//````

/// title: Reference
/// description: Either a floating absolute URL or a revision-pinned Reference. A bare URL tracks the target's current revision; a pin fixes it.
schema Reference = AbsoluteUri | PinnedReference

/// title: Pinned reference
/// description: A complete exact Reference — both the target URL and a concrete revision, no other members.
schema PinnedReference {
  /// description: The target Resource's identity URL.
  uri: AbsoluteUri,
  /// description: The concrete opaque revision of the recorded target.
  revision: Revision
}

//````
## Resource records

The self-describing Bead and Link records (§2.2). `properties` holds the content the
Resource's Type governs; `metadata` holds content the Type does not — both open JSON
objects, specified inline. Storage class and provenance are returned with the record.
//````

/// title: Bead
/// description: A complete Bead — a node in the Scope. Carries its own identity, Type, revision and storage class so a record read from a stream or an export describes itself.
schema BeadRepresentation {
  /// description: Absolute canonical immutable Resource identity URL.
  id: AbsoluteHttpUrl,
  /// description: Resource-kind discriminator; always `bead` here.
  kind: "bead",
  /// description: The Resource's Type (§2.4).
  type: Reference,
  /// description: Opaque version token of this recorded state.
  revision: Revision,
  /// description: How this Resource is kept — durable, ephemeral or intrinsic (§2.6).
  storageClass: StorageClass,
  /// description: Application-authored, Type-governed object state.
  properties: { ...: any },
  /// description: Common mutable state the Type does not govern.
  metadata: { ...: any },
  /// description: Who took part in the change that made this revision, and how its period began (§3.2).
  revisionProvenance?: RevisionProvenance,
  /// description: The Bead's owned Links, when selected as a current aggregate (§2.8). OPEN: owned Links may be withdrawn from the model.
  ownedLinks?: [Reference]
}

/// title: Link
/// description: A complete directed Link — an edge from `source` to `target`. Link identity is its own Resource identity, independent of the endpoint tuple; two Links may share Type, source and target.
schema LinkRepresentation {
  /// description: Absolute canonical immutable Resource identity URL.
  id: AbsoluteHttpUrl,
  /// description: Resource-kind discriminator; always `link` here.
  kind: "link",
  /// description: The Resource's Type (§2.4).
  type: Reference,
  /// description: Opaque version token of this recorded state.
  revision: Revision,
  /// description: How this Resource is kept; a Link is as ephemeral as its most ephemeral endpoint (§2.6).
  storageClass: StorageClass,
  /// description: The Link's source endpoint Reference.
  source: Reference,
  /// description: The Link's target endpoint Reference.
  target: Reference,
  /// description: Application-authored, Type-governed object state.
  properties: { ...: any },
  /// description: Common mutable state the Type does not govern.
  metadata: { ...: any },
  /// description: Who took part in the change that made this revision (§3.2).
  revisionProvenance?: RevisionProvenance
}

/// title: Resource representation
/// description: The generic read/create/update value — a Bead or a Link, discriminated by `kind`.
schema ResourceRepresentation = BeadRepresentation | LinkRepresentation

/// title: Resource identity
/// description: The identity, Type and concrete revision recorded for a Resource — including the final revision a deletion minted. Not a live postimage; what `delete` returns.
schema ResourceIdentity {
  /// description: Absolute canonical immutable Resource identity URL.
  id: AbsoluteHttpUrl,
  /// description: Concrete opaque revision of the recorded version.
  revision: Revision,
  /// description: The Resource's Type (§2.4).
  type: Reference
}

//````
## Storage class and provenance

How a Resource is kept, and who made its current revision (§2.6, §3.2).
//````

/// title: Storage class
/// description: How a Resource is kept. Durable state is replicated and exported; ephemeral state is local and never exported; intrinsic Resources are computed by the service, not stored.
schema StorageClass =
    Durable   { versioned: boolean }
  | Ephemeral
  | Intrinsic

/// title: Revision provenance
/// description: Who took part in the change that made a revision, in what role, and when it was committed; also how the Resource's present period began. Stored with the revision, returned as `revisionProvenance`. OPEN: shape proposed (follows gastownhall/beads#7510), not yet adopted text.
schema RevisionProvenance {
  /// description: The principals who took part, each in one or more roles.
  principals?: [Principal],
  /// description: How the Resource's present period began. OPEN enum; a reader keeps a value it does not know.
  inception?: string,
  /// description: When this revision was committed.
  committedAt?: DateTime,
  /// description: A human-readable note on the change.
  message?: string,
  /// description: Open carrier for additional provenance members.
  metadata?: { ...: any }
}

/// title: Principal
/// description: An identity that took part in a change — a person, an agent or a service account. A role grants no permission; authorization is a separate decision (§9).
schema Principal {
  /// description: Absolute URI or URN naming the identity; need not be resolvable.
  id: AbsoluteUri,
  /// description: A saved label; never identity and never a matching key.
  displayName?: string,
  /// description: Open set of roles held — `initiator`, `contributor`, `submitter`, …; a reader keeps one it does not know.
  roles?: [string],
  /// description: How this identity was established.
  basis: IdentityBasis,
  /// description: For a channel-authenticated identity, the service that established it.
  authority?: AbsoluteUri
}

//````
## Execution context

Request and reply context ride the headers for the five data primitives and sit in
the `Reply` body for `call` (§4.3). Provenance is supplied here, never as an operation
input.
//````

/// title: Request context
/// description: What a caller supplies alongside a request — the cause it acts under, the provenance to record, and open metadata.
schema RequestContext {
  /// description: The cause this request acts under (§4.2).
  cause?: Reference,
  /// description: Provenance the caller supplies for the revision this request makes (§3.2).
  provenance?: RevisionProvenance,
  /// description: Open caller-supplied metadata.
  metadata?: { ...: any }
}

/// title: Reply context
/// description: What a service returns alongside a response — the cause, the transaction the change committed in, and open metadata.
schema ReplyContext {
  /// description: The cause the operation was recorded under (§4.2).
  cause?: Reference,
  /// description: The transaction this change committed in; shared by every event from one commit (§4.1).
  transaction?: Reference,
  /// description: Open service-supplied metadata.
  metadata?: { ...: any }
}

//````
## Outcome, Reply and Problem

The in-band result model for `call` (§4.4, §4.5). `Result` is the callable's own
closed verdict; `Outcome` is the open envelope the protocol wraps it in — a reader
MUST tolerate an arm it does not know. The tag spelling is OPEN (§15).
//````

/// title: Result
/// description: A callable's own verdict — it succeeded with a value, or failed with its declared error. Closed; `value` and `error` are JSON Core does not look inside.
schema Result =
    success { value: any }
  | failure { error: any }

/// title: Outcome
/// description: What happened to an operation — the Result, or a protocol-reported `fault`, or an arm added by another specification. Open: a reader MUST tolerate an unknown arm, and every arm but `success` means the operation did not go as intended.
schema Outcome =
    success  { value: any }
  | failure  { error: any }
  | fault    { problem: Problem }
  | ...

/// title: Reply
/// description: What the caller of `call` gets back — the reply context paired with the outcome. The outcome is absent only before the operation settles; a settled result bead and a synchronous response both carry one.
schema Reply {
  /// description: The reply context (cause, transaction, metadata).
  context: ReplyContext,
  /// description: The settled outcome, present once the operation is settled.
  outcome?: Outcome
}

/// title: Problem
/// description: An RFC 9457 problem document — what a `fault` carries, and the body of a failing response from the five data primitives (§4.5, §13.2).
/// bdp = { id = "https://github.com/gastownhall/bdp/schemas/bdp-v0.2#/$defs/problem" }
schema Problem {
  /// description: A URI identifying the problem type.
  type: AbsoluteUri,
  /// description: Short human-readable summary of the problem type.
  title: string,
  /// description: HTTP status code for this occurrence.
  status: number,
  /// description: Machine-branchable BDP problem code (§12 enumerates them).
  code: string,
  /// description: Whether a retry can help — `never`, `after-state-change` or `after-delay`.
  retry: "never" | "after-state-change" | "after-delay",
  /// description: Human-readable explanation specific to this occurrence.
  detail?: string,
  /// description: A URI identifying the specific occurrence.
  instance?: AbsoluteUri
}

//````
## Request bodies

The inputs the write and invoke primitives carry (§5, §13).
//````

/// title: Create request
/// description: The body of `create`, uniform over kind — one shape for a Bead or a Link, discriminated by `kind`. The typed per-kind entry points are corelib callables, not Core.
schema CreateRequest {
  /// description: Which Resource shape to admit.
  kind: ResourceKind,
  /// description: The new Resource's Type (§2.4).
  type: Reference,
  /// description: Caller-proposed identity, when the caller allocates it (§5.4).
  id?: Reference,
  /// description: Source endpoint; required when `kind` is `link`.
  source?: Reference,
  /// description: Target endpoint; required when `kind` is `link`.
  target?: Reference,
  /// description: Initial Type-governed state.
  properties?: { ...: any },
  /// description: Initial common metadata.
  metadata?: { ...: any }
}

/// title: JSON Patch operation
/// description: One RFC 6902 operation. In `update` the paths are limited to `/properties` and `/metadata` (§3.3).
schema JsonPatchOp {
  op: "add" | "remove" | "replace" | "move" | "copy" | "test",
  path: string,
  value?: any,
  from?: string
}

/// title: JSON Patch
/// description: The `update` body — an ordered list of JSON Patch operations.
schema JsonPatch = [JsonPatchOp]

/// title: Call request
/// description: The body of `call` — the callable's arguments, JSON Core carries and does not look inside (§6.1).
schema CallRequest {
  /// description: The callable's arguments.
  arguments: { ...: any }
}

/// title: Async result bead
/// description: The result bead an asynchronous `call` returns at its Location; settled when it holds an outcome (§6.5).
schema AsyncResultBead = BeadRepresentation

//````
## Events

Each Scope has a built-in **changefeed** EventSource whose events report changes to
its Resources (§7.3). The change event is Core's own wire shape for what the data
primitives did (§7.2); an EventSource's own declaration — event schema, retention,
cardinality — belongs to Types, not Core (§7.1).
//````

/// title: Change event
/// description: Core's report of one committed change to one Resource. Links are their own subjects. Revision and provenance ride on the event, beside `data` and not inside it (§7.2).
schema ChangeEvent {
  /// description: Identifies the event.
  id: AbsoluteHttpUrl,
  /// description: The event's position in its stream.
  ordinal: Ordinal,
  /// description: What happened — created, updated or deleted.
  type: EventType,
  /// description: The EventSource the event was read from.
  source: AbsoluteHttpUrl,
  /// description: The URL of the Resource that changed.
  subject: AbsoluteHttpUrl,
  /// description: The changed Resource's kind.
  subjectKind: ResourceKind,
  /// description: The changed Resource's Type.
  subjectType: Reference,
  /// description: The revision this change minted.
  revision: Revision,
  /// description: The prior live revision, on an `updated` or a revived `deleted` subject.
  previousRevision?: Revision,
  /// description: The transaction this change committed in; shared across one commit.
  transaction?: Reference,
  /// description: The cause the change was made under (§4.2).
  cause?: Reference,
  /// description: When the change was committed.
  time: DateTime,
  /// description: Provenance of the reported revision, so a reader need not read again (§7.2).
  revisionProvenance?: RevisionProvenance,
  /// description: The change itself — the complete new state or a patch; absent on a deletion.
  data?: any
}

/// title: Pull request
/// description: The input to `pull` — which stream, where to start, how much, and which representation. `after` (a cursor) and `from` (a fresh start point) are mutually exclusive (§7.4).
schema PullRequest {
  /// description: The EventSource to read; the changefeed by default.
  source: Reference,
  /// description: A cursor from an earlier `pull` to resume from.
  after?: Cursor,
  /// description: A fresh start point without a cursor — the oldest retained event, or now.
  from?: Revision,
  /// description: Caps the number of events returned.
  limit?: number,
  /// description: Ask for complete states or patches.
  representation?: EventRepresentation
}

/// title: Pull page
/// description: One finite page of a stream — the floor every service offers: one request, one page, nothing held open (§7.4).
schema PullPage {
  /// description: The events in this page, in stream order.
  events: [ChangeEvent],
  /// description: Where to resume; opaque, bound to this stream and selection.
  cursor: Cursor,
  /// description: The stream head, so a reader can tell whether it has caught up.
  head?: Revision
}

//````
## Export

Ruled (Donna, 2026-11-11): the export **format** is defined here in Core — it is too
essential to the model to live elsewhere. The producing and consuming operations are
corelib callables (Melinda's to propose), but the format they move is Core's.

**Container (ruled).** An export is **JSON Lines**: a first record, then one Resource
record per line, then a last record. Each Resource line is **literally the result of
`get`** — a complete `ResourceRepresentation` (§2.2), never a patch. An export carries
the **latest revision only**; exporting revision history is a named later addition, and
a patch form will likely follow delta change events. It is the current durable state the
`create`/`update`/`delete` stream adds up to, cut at a cursor a reader resumes the
changefeed from (§10 item 12, §11). Boundary (§11): no callables, no call-record, no
ephemeral or intrinsic Resources. A starting point, open to revision.

**Framing records (ruled minimal; names and spelling proposed).** The first record opens
the file with the Scope and the cursor it was cut at; the last record closes it with a
count of the Resource records. A reader **refuses a file that ends without the last
record** (proposed, carried from v0.1), so a truncated export is never taken for a whole
one. Each line self-describes by `kind` — `bead` / `link` for a Resource, a framing kind
for the two ends. Levels, per-family counts and a digest stay out until ruled.
//````

/// title: Export — begin record
/// description: The first line of an export (ruled minimal; name/spelling proposed). Names the Scope and the cursor the export was cut at, so a consumer resumes the changefeed from exactly here. Told apart from a Resource line by its `kind`.
schema ExportBegin {
  /// description: Framing discriminator for the opening line. PROPOSED spelling.
  kind: "export-begin",
  /// description: The exported Scope.
  scope: AbsoluteHttpUrl,
  /// description: The cursor the export was cut at; where a consumer resumes the changefeed.
  cursor: Cursor
}

/// title: Export — end record
/// description: The last line of an export (ruled minimal; name/spelling proposed). Carries a count of the Resource records, so a truncated file — one that ends without this line — is detectable and never taken for whole (refusal proposed, carried from v0.1). Levels, per-family counts and a digest are deferred.
schema ExportEnd {
  /// description: Framing discriminator for the closing line. PROPOSED spelling.
  kind: "export-end",
  /// description: Number of Resource records between the begin and end lines.
  count: number
}

//````
## Appendix — the `///` metadata vocabulary

Metadata is **one namespaced record per backend**. A `///` field is either a plain
string (`key: text`) or a typed record / array / scalar value (`key = { … }`), read
by the v0.2 `=` value reader. The namespace is the single top-level key — `bdp`,
`openapi`, `jsonschema`, `go`, … — so there are **no dotted keys and no key-grammar
change**: the structure lives in the value.

Cross-cutting doc fields use the plain-string form: `title:` and `description:` (on a
schema, and on each field inside a record), consumed by every backend.

- **`bdp`** — protocol markers: `{ kind = "primitive", primitive = "get", id = "<uri>" }`
- **`doc`** — reference-rendering mode: `{ style = "http" }` documents an operation as
  HTTP/REST (Method · Request Headers · Request Entity Body · Response Headers · Response
  Entity Body · Errors); `{ style = "rpc" }` (the default) keeps the signature-style block
  used for Types, Callables and EventStreams.
- **`jsonschema`** — refinements Lumen's base types don't carry: `{ format = "date-time" }`
  · `{ pattern = "…" }` · `{ minLength = 1 }` · `{ closed = false }`
- **`openapi`** — projection control:
  `{ operationId, method, pathItem, tags = [ … ],`
  `  request = { contentType },`
  `  params = { <name> = { in, schema, required } },`
  `  paramGroups = [ "requestContext", "replyContext" ],`
  `  success = { status, contentType },`
  `  problem = { status = [ … ], contentType = "application/problem+json" },`
  `  responses = [ { status, contentType, schema, description } ],`
  `  responseHeaders = { Location = { schema } } }`

`paths` is empty (URLs are discovered); signatures live in `components.pathItems`.
`requestContext` → `BDP-Cause / BDP-Provenance / BDP-Metadata` request headers;
`replyContext` → `BDP-Cause / BDP-Transaction / BDP-Metadata` reply headers.
//````
